{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/record-3/week-joined-packet-1.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/record-3/week-joined-packet-1.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 37,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor technology.\\nHuang's presence is particularly notable for Nvidia, given the company's position at the center of the global AI chip market and the ongoing U.S.-China technology relationship. Investors will be watching whether discussions around AI and semiconductor trade create any signals relevant to Nvidia's access to the Chinese market, export restrictions, or the broader outlook for AI infrastructure demand.\\nIntel vs. TSMC: One Chip Stock Controls 72.5% of the Foundry Market. The Other Is Mounting a Comeback.\\nWhile the dinner itself does not guarantee any policy changes, Sept. 24 could provide an important backdrop for Nvidia stock as the market weighs the potential implications of renewed U.S.-China engagement.\\nNvidia is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $5.5 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\\nNvidia stock has gained steady momentum in recent sessions, with the shares now sitting just 4% below their 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. The stock is up 22% year-to-date (YTD) and 28% over the past 52 weeks, while its five-day gain stands at 7%, reflecting an acceleration in recent trading. Although in the red this morning, Nvidia has otherwise advanced in each of the past five sessions through Sept. 22, including gains of 2.5% on Sept. 17, 1.3% on Sept. 18, 2.3% on Sept. 21, and 0.66% yesterday, Sept. 22. It surged marginally in the last session, closing at $228.87.\\nNvidia's recent stock advance has been supported by several developments reinforcing the strength of the AI infrastructure cycle. The company's latest quarterly results showed revenue growth of 106%, while management has continued to signal strong demand for its next-generation AI systems.\\nMeanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong. The stock is also benefiting from renewed optimism around the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms as customers continue expanding AI infrastructure.\\nIn addition, expectations surrounding the upcoming Trump-Xi engagement have added another potential catalyst, with investors watching for any developments that could improve Nvidia's ability to sell AI chips in China.\\nNVDA trades at 24.04 times forward earnings and 24.81 times sales, which is currently a premium compared to industry peers.\\nNvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\nThe company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\\nNvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\\nFurthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\\nIt's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\\nBernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon reiterated a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on NVDA stock this week while maintaining a $400 price target. The target was unchanged from Rasgon's earlier revision, when Bernstein raised it from $315 to $400 on Aug. 27, underscoring the analyst's continued confidence in Nvidia's earnings and AI-driven growth outlook.\\nFurthermore, Rothschild & Co Redburn analyst Timm Schulze-Melander maintained a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on Nvidia with a $325 price target.\\nOverall, NVDA stock has a consensus \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating. Of the 50 analysts covering it, 46 advise a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" three suggest a \\\"Moderate Buy,\\\" and one offers a \\\"Strong Sell\\\" rating.\\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $325.88, indicating a potential upside of 45%. The Street-high target price of $515 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 129%.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 46,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Set For 40% Surge: 3 Catalysts That Could Drive the Next Big Move\",\n   \"text\": \"NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 54,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Director Is Cashing Out $300M. Should You Be Worried?\",\n   \"text\": \"NVDA director Mark Stevens sold roughly $300M in shares outside a prearranged plan, yet the stock still gained 23% year to date.\\nMultiple NVIDIA insiders including Jensen Huang and CFO Colette Kress also sold shares in September, suggesting broad scheduled liquidity rather than a bearish signal.\\nQ2 fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year to $96B, making the director's trim a portfolio move, not a business warning.\\nAn NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) director sold a block of stock worth roughly $300 million in early September, and the awkward part is that the stock didn't seem to care. NVIDIA closed at $228.87 on September 22, 2026, and shares are up 7.87% over the past week and 23.01% year to date. This is the latest in a run of insider disposals.\\nAn insider sale carries minimal information and substantial noise. The discipline is separating what a Form 4 filing actually discloses from what a bearish market wants it to mean, especially at a $5.53 trillion company whose chief executive just told you demand runs well above supply.\\nWhat the Filing Discloses, and What It Does Not\\nDirector Mark A. Stevens recorded disposals through a family trust between August 31 and September 4, 2026, with individual trades priced roughly between $220 and $234. Verified blocks include 447,400 shares at $220.0959 and four separate 300,000-share trades on September 2.\\nThe filing was not reported under a prearranged trading plan. A prearranged plan lets an insider schedule sales in advance, removing discretion and timing questions.\\nForm 4 filings disclose no motive. The weighted average prices reflect a block worked across the market, which is how a stake this size gets moved without dislocating the stock.\\nFamily trust sales routinely serve estate planning, diversification, and tax timing. A director who has held since the early days sits on a position no sensible risk manager would leave concentrated.\\nThe insider dataset does not provide the remaining ownership stake, so the residual is unknown but almost certainly still very large. That matters because a director who has held since the early days would retain a substantial position even after a sale of this size.\\nThe stock has run hard. Trimming into a 7.87% weekly move with a 23.01% year-to-date gain is what a long-term holder with an enormous embedded gain does.\\nA large sale outside a prearranged plan reads as a sentiment signal. It landed as Bloomberg wrote that NVIDIA's stock is flashing a warning sign as valuation falls, and Reddit chatter softened, with a bearish sentiment score of 38 on the stocks subreddit at midday September 22.\\nThe louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \\\"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\\\"\\nSales carry limited signal because there are many reasons to sell and essentially one reason to buy, which is why buys are more informative.\\nOther insiders disposed in the same window. Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, EVP Debora Shoquist and general counsel Timothy Teter all recorded disposals on September 16 or 17, and director Tench Coxe recorded 500,000-share dispositions on July 1, August 5 and September 2. This looks like scheduled liquidity across the board.\\nWatch two things: whether a further notice of proposed sale appears signaling more to come, and whether any insider buying shows up on the other side. Either signal would carry more information than the disposals themselves.\\nBull: the business is compounding. Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue was $96.22 billion, up 105.8% year over year, with a Q3 guide of $108 billion at the midpoint and a preliminary fiscal 2028 growth expectation of approximately 70%. An insider trimming a decades-old position tells you about his portfolio rather than about next quarter's orders.\\nBear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\nCustomer demand evidence decides the outcome. Track hyperscaler capital-spending guidance and the Vera Rubin ramp. Those tell you whether the compounding continues. The Form 4 tells you a wealthy man got liquid. If you would rather look past the chipmaker itself, the power, cooling, and networking suppliers behind the same buildout are worth knowing, and we profiled seven of them in a free AI infrastructure report.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 78,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Marvell vs. NVIDIA: One AI Stock Looks Like the Better Buy Now\",\n   \"text\": \"Both Marvell Technology, Inc. MRVL and its larger counterpart, NVIDIA Corporation NVDA, are benefiting from the rising Data Center demand amid the ongoing artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure boom. While both companies delivered strong quarterly results, differences in profitability, AI exposure, and outlook warrant a closer look when determining which stock to buy now.\\nMarvell's AI Momentum Powers Record Growth\\nGiven Marvell's scale, the company posted substantial revenue growth in its latest quarterly results. Revenues for the fiscal second quarter of 2027 reached a record $2.739 billion, up 37% year over year, according to the company's Aug. 27 press release. With Marvell supplying vital infrastructure for data center systems, its Data Center revenues grew even faster, up 46% year over year.\\nFor the fiscal third quarter of 2027, management expects revenues to reach $3.15 billion, plus or minus 5%. At the midpoint, it represents roughly 15% sequential growth. Management also remained optimistic about revenue growth throughout fiscal year 2027 and 2028. This suggests that Marvell's strong performance goes beyond the latest quarter, with management increasing its forward expectations.\\nMarvell's Chairman and chief executive officer, Matt Murphy, said the company is seeing exceptionally strong AI-related bookings, with growth expected to accelerate further across their Data Center portfolio, supported by robust demand in Connectivity and accelerating Custom business.\\nNVIDIA's AI Boom Fuels Record Growth and Strong Profitability\\nNVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\\nNVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\\nNVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth. Profitability is also expected to remain strong, while the company's Vera Rubin platform is now in full production, positioning NVIDIA to capitalize on continued AI infrastructure demand.\\nBanking on AI and Data Center demand, both Marvell and NVIDIA delivered strong quarterly results. Still, their exposure to the AI opportunity varies significantly.\\nMarvell's growth is increasingly driven by strong data center demand. However, if capital spending by AI infrastructure companies slows down in the future, Marvell's growth could decelerate. In contrast, NVIDIA's exposure across the broader AI computing platform, along with significantly faster Data Center growth, provides greater diversification within the AI market and reduces the company's exposure to concentration risk.\\nMarvell also appears to be emphasizing revenue expansion, but its forecasted fiscal third-quarter 2027 non-GAAP gross margin of 57.5-58.5% is below the reported 58.9% in the fiscal second quarter. This suggests that the expected revenue increase may not translate into proportional earnings, a concern for investors to monitor. In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.\\nMoreover, NVIDIA appears more efficient than Marvell at generating profits from shareholders' equity. This is because NVIDIA's return on equity (ROE) of 96% exceeds Marvell's ROE of 17.9%.\\nTherefore, NVIDIA's stronger growth, profitability, and ROE make it a more compelling investment option now than Marvell. Additionally, NVIDIA appears more attractively valued than Marvell. Per the price/earnings ratio, NVDA trades at 24.39 forward earnings compared with MRVL's forward earnings multiple of 62.25.\\nNVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Marvell has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here .\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 115,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"title\": \"What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\",\n   \"text\": \"What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\\nHow To Act On TXN?\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 121,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\\n\\\"Now, compute is revenue,\\\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\nWhen might it happen?\\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 122,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Micron vs. NVIDIA: 1 AI Stock to Buy Now and 1 to Watch\",\n   \"text\": \"Micron vs. NVIDIA: 1 AI Stock to Buy Now and 1 to Watch\\nMicron's Q3 revenues reached $41.46B, driven by strong demand for high-bandwidth memory chips.\\nNVIDIA's Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89B, while total revenues jumped 106% year over year.\\nNVIDIA's 63.7% net margin and 96% ROE exceed Micron's 55.9% and 72.5%, respectively.\\nThe rising demand for memory and Data Center infrastructure amid the artificial intelligence (AI) boom benefited both Micron Technology ( MU Quick Quote MU - Free Report ) and NVIDIA Corporation ( NVDA Quick Quote NVDA - Free Report ) . Here is a look at how the two stocks compare after their latest earnings reports and which one presents a more compelling buying opportunity.\\nStrong HBM Demand Drives Micron’s Growth and Outlook\\nAccording to Micron’s June 24 press release, the company’s revenues for the fiscal third quarter of 2026 came in at $41.46 billion, significantly higher than $23.86 billion in the previous quarter and $9.3 billion recorded a year earlier.\\nThe company’s top-line growth was driven by robust demand for Micron’s cutting-edge high-bandwidth memory (HBM) chips. Demand for HBM, which is widely used in AI servers, remains strong due to its capability to competently manage complex AI workloads while offering improved power efficiency.\\nLooking ahead, Micron expects revenues to rise to around $50 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026. The company also projects a healthy gross margin of approximately 86%, supported by strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\\nData Center Growth Drives NVDA’s Revenues and Profitability\\nNVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.\\nAlong with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.\\nLooking ahead, NVIDIA projects $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in revenues for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, signaling continued sequential growth. Profitability is also expected to remain robust, and with the Vera Rubin platform entering full production, NVIDIA is well-poised to benefit from the next phase of demand for AI infrastructure.\\nMicron or NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is a Better Buy Now?\\nDriven by strong HBM demand, Micron delivered strong revenue growth and profitability, while robust Data Center growth has boosted NVIDIA’s revenues and profitability. However, unlike Micron’s growth, which is much more concentrated in the memory cycle, NVIDIA’s broader AI infrastructure exposure gives the Jensen Huang-led company a stronger growth outlook and greater earnings visibility.\\nAdditionally, NVIDIA’s 63.7% net profit margin, compared with Micron’s 55.9%, indicates greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\\nMoreover, NVIDIA appears more efficient than Micron at generating profits from shareholders’ equity. This is because NVIDIA’s return on equity (ROE) of 96% exceeds Micron’s ROE of 72.5%.\\nOverall, NVIDIA’s broader AI exposure, stronger margins, and higher ROE make it a more compelling investment opportunity now than Micron. New investors may consider waiting for Micron’s upcoming earnings release on Sept. 30, after the market close, to assess growth and opportunity before making an informed investment decision.\\nNVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Micron has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today’s Zacks Rank #1 stocks here .\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 177,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"title\": \"What Could Stop NVIDIA Stock Over The Next Six Months?\",\n   \"text\": \"What Could Stop NVIDIA Stock Over The Next Six Months?\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock has returned 32% over the past six months, well ahead of the S&P 500. That run began near the bottom of its worst fall of the past year. In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs. At $303 billion of sales over the past year, each margin point is worth about $3 billion. The table shows what could stall that run over the next six months and when each event lands.\\nnow, and at least through the end of fiscal 2028\\nNVIDIA reports fiscal Q3 2027 results\\nNVIDIA’s Fiscal Q3 Report Lands On November 17\\nNVIDIA reports its fiscal Q3 2027 results after the market closes on November 17, 2026. Management has guided revenue to $108 billion , plus or minus 2%.\\nThe company began production shipments of Vera Rubin, its newest data center system, in August 2026. Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. On November 17, watch whether revenue clears the low end of that guide, about $106 billion. The same report will show how gross margin held up against rising memory costs.\\nThe Memory Costs Squeezing NVIDIA’s Margin\\nManagement expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.\\nNVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028. Management expects those higher prices to lift margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028. On November 17, watch fiscal Q3 2027 gross margin against 73.5%, the low end of management’s gross margin guide for that quarter. A reading below that would mean the margin is falling faster than management planned.\\nHigher prices can lift the margin off its low. They cannot add chips when the whole supply chain is already stretched.\\nCan NVIDIA’s Supply Chain Build Enough Chips?\\nNVIDIA cannot build enough for now. Management expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said its entire supply chain is stretched.\\nManagement’s preliminary outlook is for revenue to grow about 70% in fiscal 2028. Management called that outlook supply-constrained. Demand without that limit would be a lot higher, management said.\\nManagement expects sales to the largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027, as Vera Rubin supply grows. On November 17, watch whether management keeps its fiscal 2028 growth outlook. A lower figure could mean tighter supply or softer demand. Note which one management names.\\nIf you hold NVIDIA stock, you will hold it through all three of these events. The stock has fallen hard within the past year.\\nHow Much NVIDIA Stock Can You Hold Through This?\\nOnly as much as you could leave alone through a 20% drop, the stock’s worst fall in the past year . NVIDIA stock took that loss between October 29, 2025, and March 30, 2026. Over that fall, a $10,000 stake became about $7,980.\\nReport days add swings in both directions. NVIDIA stock gained 7.0% over the two days around the August 2026 earnings call. It lost 4.1% over the two days around the February 2026 earnings call.\\nIf a loss of that size would push you to sell, your position is too large for these dates. Size it so a repeat of that fall is a loss you could sit through. NVIDIA needs its margin to stop where management said it would, and revenue to land near its guide.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 232,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"title\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\",\n   \"text\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\\nA $10,000 holding in NVIDIA (NVDA) stock three months ago was worth about $11,700 as of September 25, 2026. Yet against profits, the shares still trade near the low end of their ten-year range. If you have been waiting to buy NVIDIA, have you already missed it?\\nHave You Missed The Low Multiple On NVIDIA Stock?\\nNot if you judge it by earnings against its own past. The price-to-earnings ratio, or P/E, is the share price divided by a year of profit per share. NVIDIA trades at 28.1 times earnings , above the 22.1 for the S&P 500. Over the past ten years its P/E has ranged from 19.6 to 143.1, and today it is near the bottom.\\nBut a low P/E only stays low if profit holds up. NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%. If margins slipped back toward that average, the same price would buy less profit.\\nCash looks less comfortable than earnings. Against operating cash flow, the cash the business brings in from running itself, the stock costs 40.4 times. The S&P 500 costs 14.5 times. Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch. On earnings or on cash, a price built on peak margins needs profit growth to continue.\\nHow Much Revenue Is NVIDIA Stock Counting On?\\nThe price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\\nThat expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\\nThe newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. Management says supply, not demand, is the limit. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\nGross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory. The next report has to beat NVIDIA’s own revenue forecast. The stock has not always taken its reports well.\\nNVIDIA’s Fiscal Q3 2027 Results Land in November\\nNVIDIA is expected to report on or around November 17, 2026. That report covers fiscal Q3 2027, and it will show whether revenue matches what the price appears to assume.\\nThe stock’s record after reports runs both ways. Over the last six reports, NVIDIA shares fell after four and rose after two, measured over two trading days. The biggest drop was 9.4%, after the February 25, 2026, report. The biggest gain was 3.8%, after the August 26, 2026, report.\\nA broad market sell-off is a separate risk. During the 2025 tariff shock, NVIDIA stock fell 32% from peak to low, against 19% for the S&P 500.\\nIf fiscal Q3 2027 revenue lands below $111.3 billion, the price will have asked for more than NVIDIA delivered. Buying now would then prove early. Suppose revenue clears $111.3 billion and gross margin comes in at 74% or better. The business will then have kept pace with the run. Buying at today’s price would not have been too late.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"joined_lines\": [\n  {\n   \"line\": \"T-085\",\n   \"members\": [\n    {\n     \"article\": 37,\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"article\": 46,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"excludes China Data Center compute revenue\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\",\n      \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"article\": 54,\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Midpoint of guidance\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue was $96.22 billion, up 105.8% year over year, with a Q3 guide of $108 billion at the midpoint and a preliminary fiscal 2028 growth expectation of approximately 70%.\",\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year to $96B, making the director's trim a portfolio move, not a business warning.\"\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"article\": 115,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"company total, plus or minus 2%\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"article\": 121,\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 third quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company outlook; about 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"article\": 122,\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Plus or minus 2%\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Looking ahead, NVIDIA projects $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in revenues for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, signaling continued sequential growth. Profitability is also expected to remain robust, and with the Vera Rubin platform entering full production, NVIDIA is well-poised to benefit from the next phase of demand for AI infrastructure.\"\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"article\": 177,\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; company-wide; results due 17 November 2026\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA reports its fiscal Q3 2027 results after the market closes on November 17, 2026. Management has guided revenue to $108 billion , plus or minus 2%.\",\n      \"On November 17, watch whether revenue clears the low end of that guide, about $106 billion.\"\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"line\": \"T-086\",\n   \"members\": [\n    {\n     \"article\": 78,\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company total, company outlook\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth.\",\n      \"In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.\"\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"article\": 232,\n     \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"company guidance\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\"\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"line\": \"T-089\",\n   \"members\": [\n    {\n     \"article\": 177,\n     \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"about 20%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management expectation; share of data center revenue\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\"\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"article\": 232,\n     \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"about 20%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management expectation\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\"\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"line\": \"T-090\",\n   \"members\": [\n    {\n     \"article\": 115,\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"expected bottom\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"article\": 177,\n     \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management expects this to be the low point, caused by higher memory prices\",\n     \"source\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs.\",\n      \"Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.\"\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/record-3/week-joined-packet-1.json\\\")); [print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"]) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in (115,121)]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/record-3/week-joined-packet-1.json\\\")); [print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"]) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in (115,121)]'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 115 \n What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\nHow To Act On TXN?\nARTICLE 121 \n Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\n\"Now, compute is revenue,\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\nWhen might it happen?\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"line\":\"T-085\",\"mark\":\"same\",\"periods\":{\"37\":\"written\",\"46\":\"implied\",\"54\":\"implied\",\"115\":\"implied\",\"121\":\"implied\",\"122\":\"written\",\"177\":\"written\"},\"note\":\"Each article describes NVIDIA’s $108 billion third-quarter revenue outlook. Articles 46, 54, 115 and 121 place it after fiscal Q2 2027 without writing the guidance’s fiscal year.\"}\n{\"line\":\"T-086\",\"mark\":\"same\",\"periods\":{\"78\":\"written\",\"232\":\"written\"},\"note\":\"Both articles give NVIDIA’s fiscal Q3 2027 revenue guidance as $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\"}\n{\"line\":\"T-089\",\"mark\":\"same\",\"periods\":{\"177\":\"written\",\"232\":\"written\"},\"note\":\"Both say management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\"}\n{\"line\":\"T-090\",\"mark\":\"same\",\"periods\":{\"115\":\"implied\",\"177\":\"written\"},\"note\":\"Both say NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71%–72% in fiscal Q4. Article 115 places it after fiscal Q2 2027; article 177 writes fiscal Q4 2027.\"}"}}
